{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2095","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2095","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2095\",\"parent_revision\":\"5fc76e299fb8fe1f15067709884f47e1f3ac5f66\",\"root_revision\":\"666095a1bd2f2de88c370bb5ff27b13dd085513759a02da14b079d22648b4880\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n**Status**: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS\\\\n\\\\n| Input | Status | Notes |\\\\n|---|---|---|\\\\n| Functional intent | PRESENT | New `export --to-repo` subcommand + `list --source repo` |\\\\n| Scope boundaries | PRESENT | CLI subcommand + optional Stop hook + SKILL.md update |\\\\n| External interfaces | DERIVABLE | `session_query.py` script, SKILL.md frontmatter schema (lines 56-68), `.gitignore` patterns |\\\\n| Constraints | PARTIAL | Privacy/PII opt-in mechanics unspecified; sessions contain verbatim user messages |\\\\n| Existing system context | PRESENT | Script at `.claude/skills/session-historian/scripts/session_query.py`; path constants `PROJECTS_DIR`, `CACHE_DIR`, `SUMMARIES_DIR`, `DB_PATH` all confirmed |\\\\n| Verification expectations | PRESENT | Measurable signal provides exact test command |\\\\n\\\\n**Gaps requiring resolution before implementation:**\\\\n1. Privacy opt-in mechanic: who decides which sessions are committed? Manual per-session, automatic on Stop, or project-level flag?\\\\n2. `--source repo` flag: must the DuckDB index be extended with a `source` column, or does `list` scan `.claude/sessions/` directly?\\\\n3. Sensitive-content policy: verbatim user messages may include secrets or PII \u2014 is redaction in scope or is that the committer's responsibility?\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **project-portable session memory file committable to git via session-historian** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Research entry: ./research/context-management/claude-brain.md \u2014 pattern: Single-file portable memory at .claude/mind.mv2\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-05-02\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-05-04)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n7/10 \u2014 Addresses a real onboarding gap: session knowledge is lost on machine change or team handoff. Unblocked by other items. Does not block other items. P1 rating is appropriate; not urgent enough for P0.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: none\\\\n- Bottleneck: team onboarding and cross-machine continuity \u2014 new contributors and CI agents must re-discover decisions already made in prior sessions\\\\n- Affects: all developers using session-historian who work across machines or in teams\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- New contributors cloning the repo get a queryable record of prior decisions, debugging outcomes, and architectural choices without asking the original author\\\\n- Session memory survives machine reinstalls or reassignment to a different developer\\\\n- Enables session-historian to function as a cross-machine knowledge base, not just a single-developer recall tool\\\\n- Aligns with the Claude Brain portability pattern already documented in `./research/context-management/claude-brain.md`\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nAfter running the export command, a file appears at `.claude/sessions/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<session-id-prefix>.md` inside the repository. The file uses the same frontmatter schema currently written to `~/.claude/kaizen/session-summaries/<session-id>.md` (fields: `source_type`, `session_id`, `project`, `date_range`, `user_message_count`, `assistant_turns`, `confidence`, `generated_at`). The file is tracked by git (not in `.gitignore`). Running `session_query.py list --source repo` on a machine that has never run the session shows the exported summary in its output without touching `~/.claude/kaizen/`.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\nThe target state has three observable components:\\\\n\\\\n1. A new `export` subcommand in `session_query.py` that accepts `<session-id>` and `--to-repo` flag and writes a summary to `<repo-root>/.claude/sessions/`.\\\\n2. A `list` subcommand `--source repo` flag that reads from `<repo-root>/.claude/sessions/` instead of (or in addition to) the DuckDB index at `~/.claude/kaizen/session-index.duckdb`.\\\\n3. An updated `SKILL.md` that documents the optional in-repo location alongside the user-home location.\\\\n\\\\nNo new Python dependencies are required \u2014 the summary structure and writing logic already exist for the user-home path. The path constant `SUMMARIES_DIR` in `session_query.py` line 70 is the only location that needs a conditional branch for the repo target.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `session_query.py export <session-id> --to-repo` writes a `.md` file to `<repo-root>/.claude/sessions/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<session-id-prefix>.md` with the frontmatter schema documented in SKILL.md lines 56-68.\\\\n2. The written file passes a frontmatter parse check: `source_type`, `session_id`, `project`, `date_range` fields are all present and non-empty.\\\\n3. `session_query.py list --source repo` lists the file written in criterion 1 on a machine where the DuckDB index does not contain that session.\\\\n4. `.claude/sessions/` is not listed in `.gitignore`, confirming files written there are trackable by git.\\\\n5. Running the export command twice for the same session is idempotent \u2014 produces no duplicate file and exits without error.\\\\n6. SKILL.md documents the `export --to-repo` subcommand and the `list --source repo` flag.\\n\\n### Questions for Human\\n\\n- **Privacy opt-in**: Sessions contain verbatim user messages (fidelity rule enforced in SKILL.md lines 110-116). Should `export --to-repo` require an explicit `--confirm-public` flag to acknowledge that message content will be committed to git? Or is this left to the developer's discretion?\\\\n- **Stop hook integration**: The item mentions \\\"a new optional session-summary writer (extension of session-historian or a Stop hook)\\\". Should the Stop hook automatically export to repo when a project-level opt-in is set (e.g., in `.claude/settings.json`)? Or is manual `export --to-repo` the only mechanism?\\\\n- **`--source repo` implementation**: Should `list --source repo` scan `.claude/sessions/` directly (simpler, no DuckDB involvement) or extend the DuckDB schema with a `source` column and index in-repo sessions (queryable but higher complexity)?\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| Script | `.claude/skills/session-historian/scripts/session_query.py` \u2014 path constants (lines 67-70), summary writing logic, DuckDB DDL (lines 196-222) |\\\\n| Skill | `/session-historian` \u2014 documents frontmatter schema (lines 56-68), fidelity rules (lines 110-116), index location (lines 119-122) |\\\\n| Prior work | `./research/context-management/claude-brain.md` \u2014 source pattern: single-file portable memory at `.claude/mind.mv2` |\\\\n| Config | `.gitignore` lines 33-35 \u2014 confirms `.claude/kaizen/` is gitignored; `.claude/sessions/` is not currently excluded |\\\\n| Related item | #2096 \u2014 PostToolUse observation classification; shares the goal of making session-derived knowledge queryable and persistent |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 session-historian script and SKILL.md exist and are independently modifiable\\\\n- Blocks: None currently; a Stop hook auto-export feature (if added) would be a follow-on item\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n- Privacy/PII opt-in mechanic must be decided before implementation (see Questions for Human) \u2014 without this decision, the export command cannot be designed safely for public repos\\\\n- `--source repo` implementation approach (DuckDB-integrated vs. directory scan) must be decided; the two approaches have different complexity, testability, and downstream queryability tradeoffs\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall/Medium \u2014 The summary writing logic already exists in `session_query.py` for the user-home path (`SUMMARIES_DIR`). The `export` subcommand reuses it with a different destination path. The `list --source repo` flag adds a directory scan path. Total new code: 2-3 typer command handlers and a repo-root detection utility. SKILL.md update is minor. Estimated Small if DuckDB is not extended (directory scan only), Medium if the index schema must be updated to track and query repo-sourced sessions.\\n\\n## Source Pattern\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nClaude Brain stores all memory in a single `.claude/mind.mv2` file committable to git for instant teammate onboarding. SOURCE: ./research/context-management/claude-brain.md \u2192 Key Features \u2192 Single-File Memory Storage.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Target State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nA new optional session-summary writer (extension of session-historian or a Stop hook) can persist a structured summary into `.claude/sessions/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<session-id-prefix>.md` inside the repo, where it is:\\n\\n- Tracked by git (or explicitly gitignored per project preference, but the location supports tracking)\\n- Discoverable by other team members on clone\\n- Indexable by session-historian on any machine that clones the repo\\n\\nAdd a `session_query.py export <session-id> --to-repo` subcommand that writes the same summary structure currently used in `~/.claude/kaizen/session-summaries/` to `<repo>/.claude/sessions/`. Update SKILL.md to document the optional in-repo location alongside the user-home location.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Measurable Signal\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRun from a clean clone of the repo on a different machine:\\n\\n```bash\\n.claude/skills/session-historian/scripts/session_query.py list --source repo\\n```\\n\\nOutput lists session summaries committed under `.claude/sessions/` without touching `~/.claude/kaizen/`. File at `.claude/sessions/<date>-<session>.md` contains the same frontmatter schema documented in SKILL.md lines 56-68 (`source_type`, `session_id`, `project`, `date_range`, etc.).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Current State\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nSession knowledge in this repo lives in two non-portable locations:\\n\\n1. Raw transcripts at `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/<session-id>.jsonl` \u2014 per-user home directory, never committed, lost on machine change.\\n2. Cached AI summaries at `~/.claude/kaizen/session-summaries/<session-id>.md` and DuckDB index at `~/.claude/kaizen/session-index.duckdb` \u2014 also per-user, also outside the repo.\\n\\n`.claude/skills/session-historian/SKILL.md` line 3 documents that summaries are \\\"cached at `~/.claude/kaizen/session-summaries/`\\\". Lines 121-122 confirm both the index and summaries live under `~/.claude/kaizen/`. Grep of the repo for any `.claude/sessions/` or project-relative session store returns no results \u2014 there is no in-tree, git-trackable session memory artifact a teammate could pull on `git clone`.\\n\\nResult: a new contributor cloning this repo cannot read what previous sessions decided, debugged, or attempted. Memory is bound to the original developer's home directory.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"fb46a35c84d1bad428998368fdd233ba78492efcb1410ed273d61ea060461d4a\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPtYeTg\"}"}